## 5. The integration API

The API has **two halves**: what an integration *provides* (facets) and what it may *consume* (host services). An integration implements any subset of the facets; nothing is mandatory.

The consumed half is a restricted slice of the same core API the shells use:

```rust
trait Host {
    fn read_blob(&self, project: &ProjectId, path: &str) -> Result<Vec<u8>>;
    fn write_blob(&self, project: &ProjectId, path: &str, bytes: &[u8], expect: Expect) -> Result<()>;
    fn list(&self, project: &ProjectId, dir: &str) -> Result<Vec<Entry>>;
    fn clone_path(&self, project: &ProjectId) -> Option<PathBuf>;
    fn list_projects(&self, filter: LifecycleFilter) -> Vec<RegistryEntry>;
    fn status(&self, project: &ProjectId) -> ProjectStatus;
    fn resolve_type(&self, path: &str) -> TypeId;
    fn textual_merge3(&self, base: Option<&str>, ours: &str, theirs: &str) -> MergeOutcome;
}
```

**What's excluded is a safety property, not tidiness.** `sync`, `rewind`, branching, acquisition, `set_lifecycle` and `set_metadata` are lifecycle acts belonging to the user through a shell. An integration able to call `sync` could push on behalf of a project deliberately set to local-only, silently breaking D19/D23 — the same reasoning that keeps credentials inside `read_blob`.

`status` is present because D30 needs it: TODO archival runs only while a project is **Synced**. `resolve_type` is present despite having no current caller — read-only introspection is cheap to expose and can't be misused, and mutation is what needs justifying. `get_metadata` is *absent*: its sole justification was role→path resolution, which D37 deleted.

**Host services arrive through an injected context, never a global.** That's what makes a fake host possible in the CLI and in tests, and it's the only shape that could ever be proxied out-of-process.

### 5.1 Core-side facets

```rust
trait Merge  { fn merge3(&self, base: Option<&[u8]>, ours: &[u8], theirs: &[u8]) -> MergeOutcome; }
trait Diff   { fn diff(&self, a: &[u8], b: &[u8]) -> DiffModel; }
```

**`merge3` and `diff` are pure and receive no `Host`.** T6 is the highest-value correctness work in the project, and a merge that can perform I/O needs a mocked filesystem to test while making "identical regardless of which side is ours" much harder to guarantee, since the result could depend on ambient state. Purity makes property testing tractable and determinism structural — and it removes a reentrancy question the synchronous core would otherwise have. Only **domain operations** receive a `Host`.

